server: context on demand - the model stays loaded, its context is created by the request

A loaded model holds its weights and its context: the KV cache and the
compute buffers, sized for the window and the batch whether or not a request
runs. With --context-on-demand the server keeps the weights and holds the
context only while it works. It is created when a task needs it and freed
when no task is left, and a request says for how long it is kept after its
answer (`keep_context_ms`, in the request; --keep-context-ms for a request
that does not say), so that requests back to back do not pay a creation
each. It is the `keep_alive` of other runners applied to the context instead
of the whole model, in milliseconds. Several servers on one device then need
room only for the contexts that work at the same time.

- common: common_init_result::free_context() / init_context() and
  common_init_context_from_params(): the context is created again from the
  context params of the first creation (no fitting again), its adapters
  applied and its warmup run as common_init_from_params() does - that part
  is now common_init_context_apply(), shared by both.
- server_queue::on_idle(): a callback run by the start_loop() thread each
  time it finds no task; it returns when it wants to be called again, and
  the wait ends then instead of at the next one-second check.
- server_context: only the start_loop() thread creates and frees the
  context. process_single_task() creates it for a task that needs one
  (context_acquire); a creation that fails is answered with 503
  `unavailable_error` and the server stays up, where a failed reload after
  --sleep-idle-seconds aborts. A slot's release extends the deadline by its
  task's keep (context_keep: the latest deadline asked wins, a request never
  shortens another's); the idle callback frees the context once it has
  passed (context_idle, context_release). The slots drop their cached
  prompt with the KV cache. get_meta(), n_ctx_slot() and use_mixed_batch()
  no longer need a context, so /props, /models, /metrics, /slots and the
  tokenizer routes neither wait for one nor create one.
- /metrics: context_loaded, context_creations_total,
  context_creation_seconds_total.
- refused at load with speculative decoding or a multimodal projector, whose
  contexts are bound to the model's.

Checked on the CPU (random-weight llama and qwen3 models written by
tests/test-llama-archs -o): tools/server/tests/unit/test_context_on_demand.py
- the context freed when idle and not created by the read-only routes; one
creation per request without a keep; a keep from the request (completion
and chat completion) and from the server; a keep of 0 that does not shorten another; the same completion and
the same embedding, value for value, across creations; the cached prompt
reused within a keep and gone after it; a creation refused with 503, then
served; eight concurrent requests; /slots listed without a context; together
with --sleep-idle-seconds. With the option off the existing suites give the
same results as before the patch (test_sleep, test_basic, test_metrics,
test_slot_save, test_embedding, test_completion, test_tokenize,
test_ctx_shift, test_kv_keep_only_active: the same 83 passes and the same 46
failures, the failures being the random-weight stand-ins' content).
